Here's a simple guide for uploading a bibliography database on Overleaf:

Overleaf is an online document editing platform that allows you to easily create, edit, and publish documents in LaTeX format. The platform offers a user-friendly interface and a powerful bibliography management tool that enables you to create and cite references in your document with ease.

To upload a bibliography database on Overleaf, follow these steps:

1. Create an account and start a new project on Overleaf.com.

2. Once you've created a project, you'll be directed to the LaTeX editor dashboard. From here, you can start writing your document or upload an existing LaTeX file.

3. To add citations to your document, you first need to create a bibliography file that contains all the sources you want to reference. You can do this by adding the following command (or a similar variation) in your LaTeX code, preferably near the top of your document:

   \begin{filecontents*}{MyBibliography.bib}
   @article(author, title, journal, year)
   Title of the first source
   @book(author, title, publisher, year)
   Title of the second book
   @misc/webpage(author, title, url, year)
   Title of the third website
   @phdthesis(author, title, university, year)
   Title of the dissertation
   @report(author, title, institution, year)
   Title of the research report
   @presentation(author, title, date, organization, event)
   Title of the conference paper (presented orally)
   @manual(author, title, organization, month, year)
   Title of the manual
   @legislation(author, type, nr, year)
   Title of the act (or regulation)
   @patent(author, title, issuing_country, issuing_year, number)
   Patent title of the invention
   @map(author, title, cartographer, publisher)
   Map title
   @musicrecord(author, title (in quotes), album, label, year)
   Composer name, "Title of the song", Album Title, Label, Year
   @collection(editor, title, publisher, year)
   Editor name (Editor Title, Ed.), "Title of the work in quotes"
   @referencebook(editor, title, edition, publisher, year)
   Editor name (Editor Title, Ed.), Book Title (Edition), Publisher, Year
   @dictionary(entry, year)
   Entry title, Publisher
   @thesis(thesis_type, author, title, university, year)
   PhDthesis, Author Name, Title of the dissertation, University, Year

   Separate multiple entries by a comma and one space.

   Note: The syntax above shows the general format for creating a bibliography entry. You may need to adapt the format based on the specific source type you are referencing. For example, if you want to cite a journal article, you would replace @article(author, title, journal, year) with the appropriate structure.

4. Save your bibliography file. Your .bib file should be saved in the project directory on your local machine.

5. In your LaTeX document, add the following line at the top (or within the preamble):

   \bibliographystyle{<style>}
   Replace <style> with the name of the citation style that you want to use. Overleaf provides a range of popular citation styles that you can choose from. You can find the full list of styles included on Overleaf in the "Formats" section of the menu on the left-hand side of the editor.

6. Add citations to your document. To cite a source within your document, you can use one of the citation commands provided by the chosen citation style. For example, if you're using the APA citation style and want to cite a source called "Source Title" by "Author Name" in 2018, you would type:

   Author Name (2018) Source Title

   This creates an in-text citation that includes both the author's name and the year of publication. If you have more than two authors, separate the first name with the suffixes and a comma, e.g., "Smith, J., Jr.".

7. Sync your bibliography file with your document. Click on the "Refresh" button located in the top left corner of the bibliography file window to sync your document with the newly added bibliography entries.

That's it! You've successfully uploaded and used a bibliography database on Overleaf. Your document is now ready for submission or publication, depending on the selected option.

